p=r/'docs/MissionRules/README.md';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
s=s.replace('Each compiled damage leaf owns a runtime observation using the same manager/hit-zone API for characters, vehicles and destructible buildings.', 'Compiled damage leaves share one runtime observation per resolved native hit zone, using the same manager/hit-zone API for characters, vehicles and destructible buildings.')
a=s.index('The observation reads initial health/state and subscribes to the native health and damage-state invokers.')
b=s.index('\n\nResearch:',a)
s=s[:a]+'''The observation reads initial health/state and subscribes to the native health and damage-state invokers. A native callback immediately refreshes the shared observation and notifies only its registered condition leaves. Their cached answers are updated together before mission events run. Notifications are sent when a leaf's answer or availability changes: duplicate health/state callbacks and additional damage above an already-passing threshold do not repeatedly fire the same check. Repairs can make an answer false, and crossing the threshold again can trigger a repeatable event. Destroyed / Not destroyed still use the native damage state, not a guessed health threshold.

Entity Damage is not polled in the shared 100 ms Advance path and uses no CallLater, frame handler or delayed damage dispatch. Startup and Match entry still resolve targets, share subscriptions by native hit-zone identity (including default/named aliases), and evaluate initial answers once. During Match, callbacks drain through the existing bounded framework event queue immediately. If an event action causes further damage while that queue is already running, the new notification joins the same queue rather than recursively executing another interpreter. Distinct native state transitions can still produce distinct notifications; this does not invent a universal one-notification-per-bullet contract.

There is no damage or deletion polling and no world scan. A deleted intact target becomes unavailable when its condition is next evaluated, but deletion without a damage callback does not itself schedule a damage event. A confirmed destroyed result survives wreck cleanup; if repaired before removal, the updated result applies. Missing/unsupported/lost intact targets fail the containing condition tree before inversion, so None cannot turn an unresolved reference into success. Stop removes both native subscriptions and the framework callback, clearing observations and answer caches. No client listeners, global overrides or new RPCs are added.''' +s[b:]
s=s.replace('Logic and lifecycle are source-reviewed; in-game damage, repair, deletion and multiplayer tests were deliberately not run at the user\'s request.', 'Logic and lifecycle are source-reviewed. Optional GC_EntityDamageEventTests.c covers immediate dispatch without Advance, shared leaves, duplicate callbacks, threshold recrossing, reentrant damage and Stop cleanup; it was compiled but not executed. In-game damage, repair, deletion and multiplayer tests were deliberately not run at the user\'s request.')
p.write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
